title The head that enumerates, and the merge that tells what it merged
prompt The baton -- open the round, read the rota row, take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, reds first.
rota lap 4048, N mod 5 = 3 -- Water, the row that tastes. This tree read rows 1 and 2 today, so row 3 stands fresh here. Its discipline is closeness: taste only works up close, so read the actual file, run the actual witness, sip the actual code. That instruction found the lap's red, and nothing else would have.
think Opened cold. The roster ran 143 guards in 825s -- 139 green, 0 red, 4 gated at custody, tree_moved=no, run_verdict=ok -- so the lap opened over a clean tree rather than a wreck. Then fetched xy: five commits behind, none touching mantra/ or tally/, so the fast-forward cost my lane nothing and I re-verified nothing beyond the hot pass.
think The baton says the next real weave work is the MERGE of two weaves and the annotation showing what each side did. Half of that is stale: merge landed at 00:05 today, proven by eight claims. What remained was the charter's second cascade item -- a conflict is SHOWN, annotated with what each side did, a story rather than two opaque blobs and a refusal.
think Before writing a line I read weave.rye whole, which is the water row's own instruction. Its head says "Three operations" and lists three. The module publishes four. merge landed twelve hours and several laps earlier and the head never moved.
obs THE RED, AND WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT. tools/m/mantra_weave_merge_witness.rish greps 'pub fn merge' in that same file and passed all day. The grep asks whether the CODE is there; the head is a different claim about the same bytes, and no instrument in this tree read it. That is %481's shape one room over -- every instrument aimed at a file reads it for ONE property, so the property nobody named is the one that goes stale -- and it is %439-%441's family, a claim where no meter reads.
obs THE SHARPER HALF IS WHERE THE DEFECT SAT. A stale comment beside a function is met by a reader already inside the code. A stale module head is met by a reader who has read nothing else, and it is the only thing standing between them and a module they do not know. Same for the card %481 corrupted: read whole at the open of every lap on eight ships. The files a reader BEGINS at are the ones least able to carry an unwatched claim, and they are exactly the files whose one obvious property -- length, links, a message -- is already spoken for.
obs BOTH DIRECTIONS, NOT THE ONE THAT FIRED. mantra_weave_head_scan.sh gates head_missing and head_stale at zero. A list can be wrong two ways, and a guard watching one reports the other as green. Read from the failing side before the repair: container_seen=1 declared=4 listed=3 head_missing=1 -- merge. Replanted afterward in the control's own pen, so the refusal is proven rather than remembered.
obs INDENTATION ALONE WAS WRONG, AND THE FIRST DRAFT SHIPPED IT. I scoped the operation count to 'pub fn at four-space indent' -- and Note's four readers sit at exactly four spaces, because Note is a top-level container too. The scan read them as operations of the weave. Scoped now to after 'pub const Weave = struct {', and if that line is ever renamed the scan refuses weave_container_absent rather than counting zero and answering ok. A census that reads nothing and says nothing is wrong is indistinguishable from a healthy file, which is %463's whole lesson.
obs AND AN APOSTROPHE INSIDE A SINGLE-QUOTED AWK PROGRAM CLOSED IT. The comment line "# The module's last container" sat inside the awk string and ended the quote, so the shell parsed the awk body. sh -n caught it in one run; three bisections found it. Worth recording because the failure looked like a regex fault and was a quoting fault, two lines above.
think For annotate itself the design question was what to store. A Note carries pos, left_gen and right_gen, and DERIVES the merged generation as max of the two rather than storing a third copy. A stored copy is a second answer that can drift from the merge it describes, and a story that can disagree with its subject is worse than no story.
obs ZERO EARNS ITS PLACE TWICE. The module already reserves generation zero -- every line starts at 1 -- so zero means this side never saw the position. Zero is also even, and even means gone, so an unseen position answers present false through the same parity test every other reading uses. No special case anywhere, and the reading falls out of a law that was already written.
obs THE LOAD-BEARING CLAIM IS THE MIRROR. Claim 4 runs annotate and merge on the same two weaves and holds them to each other: same count, same order, and every note's gen() equal to the generation merge lands on at that position. That is the water row's fixed seat -- two small answers at every seam -- and it is what keeps the story honest as merge grows.
obs ANNOTATE READ 83 LINES AND PASSED THE LONG-FUNCTION SCAN. It passed only because tame_style_long_fn_one.sh resets its awk on any indented 'fn ', and annotate holds an inline struct comparator -- the blind spot a sibling ship already named. Shipping on that would have been shipping on a blind instrument, which is the fault this lap booked. Split at the natural seam into notes_from_left and notes_from_right, mirroring merge's own union_into: 47, 29, 29.
obs AND THE SPLIT CHANGED WHAT ONE CONTROL PHASE PROVES. With the bound shrunk to 8 and annotate's edge check deleted, the refusal now comes from notes_from_right's own ceiling invariant one step later -- 5 notes written plus 5 incoming against a max of 8 -- rather than from the witness reaching its OversizeUnionNotRefused return. The exit code did not change and the mechanism did, so the control's header says which one runs. The module holds the bound twice, and that phase proves the second holds when the first is gone.
obs AND THE RED I BOOKED FIRED IN MY OWN NEW FILES WITHIN THE HOUR. The control header said "TWELVE PHASES -- nine over the Rye witness, three over the head scan" and the runner said "seven breaks caught". Both were true when written and both went stale the moment I added the head phases and the ninth claim: thirteen phases, four over the head scan, three innocence legs, ten breaks. Same shape as this row exactly -- a count in prose is a claim, and nothing read it. Corrected in the control, the runner, the card and the shelf. The guard I built reads ONE file's operation list; every other count in this tree's prose is still unwatched, which is why the row names that census as a lap rather than pretending to have done it.
obs A NINTH CLAIM, ADDED BECAUSE THE WITNESS HAD AN OBVIOUS HOLE. Two branches deleting the SAME line is the most ordinary shape a real merge takes, and no claim showed it. It separates the two halves of a reading: the WORD says whether the counts match, PARITY says what they match on. Both sides moved to generation 2, so neither acted last -- agreed, and gone. Adding it cost one more hot pass, which is cheaper than shipping a witness whose gap I had already seen.
obs AND I KILLED MY OWN SHELL WITH THE FAULT I WAS WRITING ABOUT. `pkill -f "standing_equipment_run.sh"` matched the bash command line that contained those characters, so it killed the process issuing it -- exit 144. That is %475's shape in one shell command, an instrument whose subject includes itself, and the previous lap on this tree recorded the identical trap in a waiter loop. Matching by PID worked. Twice in two laps means the habit, not the command, is the fault: never select a process by a pattern your own invocation contains.
loom roster=standing_equipment_cold guards_run=143 guards_green=139 guards_red=0 guards_gated=4 wall_seconds=825 tree_moved=no
loom guard=mantra_weave_annotate tier=lap wall_seconds=10 claims=10 control_phases=13 innocence_legs=3 breaks_caught=10
loom scan=mantra_weave_head before_repair_declared=4 before_repair_listed=3 head_missing=1 after_repair_declared=5 after_repair_listed=5
loom fn_lines=annotate before=83 after=47 notes_from_left=29 notes_from_right=29 ceiling=70
file mantra/src/weave.rye Note, Reading, annotate, and the two folds beside merge's union_into; the head now lists five operations and is read by a guard.
file mantra/src/weave_annotate_witness.rye nine claims proven by doing, the fourth holding the story to the merge itself.
file tools/fixtures/m/mantra_weave_head_scan.sh the head held to the module's own pub fn declarations, both directions at zero, refusing by name.
file tools/fixtures/m/mantra_weave_annotate_control.sh twelve phases -- two innocence legs and ten breaks, three of them over the head scan.
file tools/m/mantra_weave_annotate_witness.rish the runner; it says the scan's lines before it asserts on them.
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri mantra_weave_annotate rostered tier lap.
file construction/REDS.md %506 booked and closed; the headline recount.
file construction/archive/REDS-the-property-nobody-named-rows-506.md the row folded to its own shelf; %481 landed on its own and is cited as kin.
file construction/ITINERARY.md the account, the ruling GRASS asked for, and the one seam that waits on Keaton.
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- one ruling given, one question standing. Given: %484's "Patchouli's call" is answered on the card -- keep the by-name exclusion, since an exclusion by shape is a door anything walks through by adopting the shape, and the fleet reddening until a teaching page is listed is the intended price of a countable exemption. Standing, and it is the same seam twice: annotate can say WHAT each side did and not yet WHERE a new line sits against the other side's delete, because apply hands every insert a position from the end of the counter. That story wants an anchor per insert; the identity gap wants a wider Line. Both widen a shape mantra_glow_tend_limb1 pins against Glow, so one word from you opens two stories at once. And a smaller one worth your eye: this lap found a stale enumerating head in the one module I read closely, and nothing measures how many other module heads enumerate. I named that as a lap rather than sweeping it.
